**Service Management (Systemd)**
• systemctl start <service> 	— Starts a background service.
• systemctl stop <service> 	— Stops a running service.
• systemctl restart <service> 	— Restarts a service (commonly used after changing a config file).
• systemctl enable <service> 	— Configures a service to start automatically upon system boot.
• systemctl status <service> 	— Checks whether a service is active, inactive, or failing.
• journalctl -u <service> --no-pager — Fetches the system logs specifically generated by that service.

Networking & Remote Server Management
• ssh user@ip_address 		— Connects securely to remote servers or cloud virtual machines.
• scp file.tar.gz user@ip:/path — Copies files securely between local and remote environments.
• curl -I https://example.com 	— Sends HTTP requests to test endpoints and verify if web applications are running properly.
• netstat -tuln or ss -tuln 	— Checks open ports and listening services to debug firewall or application binding issues.
• ping <host> 			— Tests basic network connectivity to another server.

Process Management & System Monitoring
• ps aux or ps -ef 	— Displays all active processes currently running on the server.
• top or htop 		— Provides an interactive, real-time view of system resources like CPU, memory, and running tasks.
• kill -9 <PID> 	— Forcefully terminates a malfunctioning or hung process using its Process ID.
• df -h 		— Displays disk space usage in a human-readable format.
• free -h 		— Displays system memory (RAM) usage.

Linux Filesystem Hierarchy
• / (Root) — The starting point of the entire directory tree.
• /etc — Configuration files for the system and applications (e.g., Nginx, SSH).
• /var/log — System and application logs; the primary destination for debugging.
• /home — Personal directories for regular users to store data and scripts.
• /root — The isolated home directory reserved for the administrator (root) user.
• /bin & /sbin — Essential system commands and binaries needed to run the OS.
• /opt — Third-party, optional software packages (e.g., GitLab, custom tools).
• /tmp — Temporary files that are automatically cleared upon system reboot.
• /proc & /sys — Virtual directories exposing kernel and process hardware data in real time.

Package Manager
A Package Manager is an automated tool that installs, updates, configures, and removes software on a Linux system.
Common Package Managers by Linux Family
Linux Distribution Family				High-Level Manager	Low-Level Tool	Package Format
Debian / Ubuntu / Mint					apt or apt-get		dpkg		.deb
RHEL / CentOS / Rocky Linux / Fedora			dnf or yum		rpm		.rpm
Alpine Linux (Popular for tiny Docker containers)	apk			apk		.apk

Essential Commands
• Update package list: 	sudo apt update  
• Install software: 	sudo apt install docker.io
• Upgrade installed 	apps: sudo apt upgrade
• Remove software: 	sudo apt remove nginx

Navigation Commands
• pwd (Print Working Directory) — Displays the absolute path of the directory you are currently in.
• ls (List) — Lists directory contents. Use ls -la to show detailed properties (permissions, size, owner) and hidden files.
• cd (Change Directory) — Switches your current workspace to a new folder path.

File & Directory Management Commands
• mkdir (Make Directory) — Creates a new folder. Use mkdir -p to create a full nested path all at once.
• touch — Creates an empty file or updates the timestamp of an existing one.
• cp (Copy) — Copies files or directories. Use cp -r to copy folders recursively.
• mv (Move) — Moves or renames files and folders.
• rm (Remove) — Deletes files or directories. Use rm -rf to forcefully delete folders and everything inside them.

Real-World Example
Imagine you need to create a deployment directory for a web app, configure a backup, and clean up temporary files on a remote server.
bash

# 1. Check where you are currently located on the server
pwd
# Output: /home/ubuntu

# 2. Create a nested project structure for a new application deployment
mkdir -p myapp/config

# 3. Move into the newly created configuration directory
cd myapp/config

# 4. Create an empty environment configuration file
touch .env

# 5. List all files in the directory to verify the hidden .env file exists
ls -la
# Output: Shows permissions, owner, and the file '.env'

# 6. Go back up one directory level to the 'myapp' root folder
cd ..

# 7. Create a backup copy of the entire config folder before making changes
cp -r config config_backup

# 8. Rename the backup folder to include the word 'old'
mv config_backup config_old

# 9. Clean up and delete the old backup folder forcefully
rm -rf config_old

Tips
• Tab Completion: Press the Tab key while typing a file or folder name. Linux will automatically finish typing it for you, saving time and preventing typos.
• The Tilde (~): Typing cd ~ or just cd instantly takes you back to your user's home directory (/home/username), no matter how deep you are in the filesystem hierarchy.


Linux Basics
============

Linux disk management commands

1. Information gathering & monitoring

Before making any changes, you must identify your drives (e.g., /dev/sda, /dev/nvme0n1) and check their current status.
• lsblk 	– Lists all block devices (disks and partitions) in a clean, tree-like layout.
• lsblk -f 	– Displays filesystems (ext4, xfs, etc.) and UUIDs alongside the block devices.
• sudo fdisk -l – Lists all connected disks, their capacities, and exact partition boundaries.
• df -h 	– Displays total, used, and available disk space for all mounted filesystems in a human-readable format (GB/MB).
• du -sh /path/to/dir – Shows the total disk space utilized by a specific directory and its contents.
• sudo blkid 	– Displays the unique UUID and attributes of all available block attributes.

2. Disk Partitioning Tools

Partitioning splits a physical disk into logical segments.
• sudo fdisk /dev/sdX 	– Launches an interactive, text-menu utility optimal for smaller drives or older MBR partitioning schemes.
• Inside fdisk: p (print table), n (new partition), d (delete), w (write changes and exit).
• sudo gdisk /dev/sdX 	– The equivalent of fdisk, but specifically designed for modern, large-capacity GPT drives.
• sudo parted /dev/sdX 	– A powerful tool that handles both MBR and GPT, commonly used for scripting or managing huge storage pools.
• sudo cfdisk /dev/sdX 	– A user-friendly, interactive curses-based text interface that lets you easily manage partitions using keyboard arrow keys.
• sudo partprobe 	– Informs the Linux kernel of recent partition table adjustments without forcing a system reboot.

3. Creating Filesystems (Formatting)

Once a partition is created (e.g., /dev/sdX1), it must be formatted with a filesystem so the operating system can write data to it.
• sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdX1 	– Formats the target partition with the Ext4 filesystem, standard for most Linux distributions.
• sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/sdX1 	– Formats the target partition with XFS, which excels at high-performance enterprise workloads and very large files.
• sudo mkfs.vfat /dev/sdX1 	– Formats the drive to FAT32, useful if the drive needs to be read natively by both Windows and Linux systems.

4. Mounting & Unmounting Filesystems

Linux maps storage devices directly into the unified directory tree rather than assigning drive letters.
• sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/target_folder – Attaches the partition filesystem to an existing directory, making its contents accessible.
• sudo umount /dev/sdX1 (or /mnt/target_folder) – Safely detaches the partition. Note: Ensure no applications are reading/writing to the folder while unmounting.
• sudo mount -a – Instantly mounts all filesystems specified in the system config file (/etc/fstab). Used heavily to test manual configurations.
Tip for Permanent Mounts: To keep a drive mounted after a reboot, you must add its configuration line to the /etc/fstab file using its UUID.

5. Disk Maintenance, Health, & Diagnostics

• sudo fsck /dev/sdX1 – Checks and repairs a disconnected or unmounted filesystem for errors.
• sudo smartctl -a /dev/sdX – Performs a comprehensive SMART health check on physical drives to look for failing sectors or hardware issues.
• sudo badblocks -v /dev/sdX – Scans a hard disk thoroughly to search for unusable physical bad blocks.
